DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The rroouuttee66dd utility is a routing daemon which supports RIP over IPv6.

     Options are:

     --aa      Enables aging of the statically defined routes.  With this op‐
             tion, any statically defined routes will be removed unless corre‐
             sponding updates arrive as if the routes are received at the
             startup of rroouuttee66dd.

     --RR _r_o_u_t_e_l_o_g
             This option makes the rroouuttee66dd to log the route change
             (add/delete) to the file _r_o_u_t_e_l_o_g.

     --AA _p_r_e_f_i_x_/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n_,_i_f_1_[_,_i_f_2_._._._]
             This option is used for aggregating routes.  _p_r_e_f_i_x_/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n spec‐
             ifies the prefix and the prefix length of the aggregated route.
             When advertising routes, rroouuttee66dd filters specific routes covered
             by the aggregate, and advertises the aggregated route
             _p_r_e_f_i_x_/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n, to the interfaces specified in the comma-sepa‐
             rated interface list, _i_f_1_[_,_i_f_2_._._._].  The characters "*", "?", and
             "[" in the interface list will be interpreted as shell-style pat‐
             tern.  The rroouuttee66dd utility creates a static route to
             _p_r_e_f_i_x_/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n with RTF_REJECT flag, into the kernel routing ta‐
             ble.

     --dd      Enables output of debugging message.  This option also instructs
             rroouuttee66dd to run in foreground mode (does not become daemon).

     --DD      Enables extensive output of debugging message.  This option also
             instructs rroouuttee66dd to run in foreground mode (does not become dae‐
             mon).

     --hh      Disables the split horizon processing.

     --ll      By default, rroouuttee66dd will not exchange site local routes for
             safety reasons.  This is because semantics of site local address
             space is rather vague (specification is still in being worked),
             and there is no good way to define site local boundary.  With --ll
             option, rroouuttee66dd will exchange site local routes as well.  It must
             not be used on site boundary routers, since --ll option assumes
             that all interfaces are in the same site.

     --LL _p_r_e_f_i_x_/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n_,_i_f_1_[_,_i_f_2_._._._]
             Filter incoming routes from interfaces _i_f_1_,_[_i_f_2_._._._].  The rroouuttee66dd
             utility will accept incoming routes that are in _p_r_e_f_i_x_/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n.
             If multiple --LL options are specified, any routes that match one
             of the options is accepted.  ::/0 is treated specially as default
             route, not “any route that has longer prefix length than, or
             equal to 0”.  If you would like to accept any route, specify no
             --LL option.  For example, with “--LL 2001:db8::/16,if1 --LL ::/0,if1”
             rroouuttee66dd will accept default route and routes in 6bone test ad‐
             dress, but no others.

     --nn      Do not update the kernel routing table.

     --NN _i_f_1_[_,_i_f_2_._._._]
             Do not listen to, or advertise, route from/to interfaces speci‐
             fied by _i_f_1_,_[_i_f_2_._._._].

     --OO _p_r_e_f_i_x_/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n_,_i_f_1_[_,_i_f_2_._._._]
             Restrict route advertisement toward interfaces specified by
             _i_f_1_,_[_i_f_2_._._._].  With this option rroouuttee66dd will only advertise
             routes that matches _p_r_e_f_i_x_/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n.

     --PP _n_u_m_b_e_r
